  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "JPY and KRW invoices are now charged at their invoice total, so ¥1,500 goes to the gateway as 1500. `npm test` passes with 8 of 8 tests, including the new ones.\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cases the currency to `jpy` for the gateway before it converts the amount. The currency registry (`src/lib/currency/registry.js`) is keyed by upper-case codes, so `jpy` was not found. `exponent()` then fell back to the default of 2 decimal places, and `toMinor(1500, 'jpy')` returned 150000. USD and EUR already use 2 decimal places, so they were unaffected. The other zero- and three-decimal currencies (VND, CLP, BHD, KWD) were exposed in the same way.\n\n**Fix:** I changed the registry so `exponent()`, `isSupported()` and `symbol()` ignore case and whitespace. That covers every caller that passes gateway-style lower-case codes, not just the charge payload. The other `toMinor` callers (refunds, payouts, coupons, dunning, proration) pass stored upper-case codes, so they behave as before. The ERP, CRM and tax-provider clients lower-case their currency codes too, but they send the record as-is and do not convert amounts.\n\n**Tests:** I added two regression tests to `test/charge.test.js`:\n- **Charge payload:** a JPY charge of 1500 and a KRW charge of 12000 are sent at those amounts, not ×100.\n- **Case-insensitive lookup:** `toMinor(1500, 'jpy')` returns 1500 and `toMinor(1.234, 'kwd')` returns 1234.\n\nCustomers who were already overcharged will need refunds, and that is not handled here.",
